35,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The financial records show that fixed costs like tax, insurance, and parking totaled over 280,000 yen. More concerningly, the fuel receipts reveal a significant imbalance in who has been paying for petrol.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It is fascinating how numbers can illuminate the surface, yet the underlying friction often stems from something less tangible. I suspect that focusing solely on the ledger might inadvertently lead us into a psychological trap.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The diary shows a massive disparity between weekday and weekend usage. More importantly, the handover state is inconsistent; finding the car with an empty tank and trash inside is unacceptable for a shared arrangement.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shion,,The fuel receipts confirm exactly what I suspected: a complete mess. Two people are paying for almost everything while someone else is barely contributing. It's not just an imbalance; it's a joke.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,I... I also noticed the diary shows a big difference between weekdays and weekends. And there are a few weekly trips that always seem to clash... it makes the scheduling feel really stressful.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Shion, let's avoid using words like ""joke"" and focus on the facts. We have a fixed cost of 280,000 yen plus fuel. The real question is how to distribute these costs fairly given the usage gaps.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, your call for composure is appreciated. However, we must be cautious. If we treat this as a mere accounting error, we risk ignoring the resentment that fuels such ""jokes."" Logic dictates we address the mood, not just the math.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, the ""mood"" is a result of the lack of standards. If the car is left dirty or empty, it creates stress for the next user. We need a system that enforces basic maintenance, not just a payment plan.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, maintenance is a side issue. We can't talk about ""standards"" when some of us are essentially subsidizing others' commutes. Let's settle the debt and the payment structure first. The numbers are the only thing that matters.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think both are important. But the scheduling is really hard. Those repeating trips always clash, and it takes so much time and energy to fix it every time. Can we find a way to make that easier?","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Daisuke, scheduling is a valid concern, but it is a symptom of the lack of a formal system. If we establish a clear payment and usage structure, the scheduling conflicts should become easier to resolve logically.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, I must gently disagree. A formal system is merely a skeleton; the spirit of the group is the flesh. If we implement a rigid structure while resentment lingers, we simply give our grievances a more official channel.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, the ""spirit"" of the group cannot exist if the basic utility of the car is compromised. A rigid structure for cleanliness and fuel is not a grievance; it is a requirement for the car to be functional for everyone.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, your philosophy is a waste of time. We aren't here to heal the ""spirit"" of the group; we're here to stop being ripped off. Let's stop talking in circles and decide how to split the costs.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,I... I don't want us to fight. But it's not just about the money. The time we spend arguing over the schedule every week is... it's exhausting. I just want it to be simpler for everyone.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Minako,,"We are circling the same points. We have fixed costs of 280,000 yen, uneven fuel contributions, and scheduling friction. To move forward, we must decide if we prioritize a flat split or a usage-based model.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, while a usage-based model seems logically sound, we must be wary. My research suggests that meticulous auditing often breeds suspicion and resentment. Let us ensure our solution doesn't turn us into accountants who despise one another.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Minako, a usage-based model is logical for costs, but it doesn't solve the handover issue. Regardless of who pays what, we must implement a mandatory checklist for fuel and cleanliness to ensure the car is actually usable.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, a checklist is just more paperwork. The real issue is the money. If we go with a usage-based model, the people who actually use the car pay for it. Simple, logical, and fair. No more guesswork.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think a usage-based model sounds fair for the money. But please, can we also find a way to fix the schedule? If we just focus on the money, we'll still be exhausted from the arguing.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
